WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.400
With a franchise as iconic as the James Bond&nbsp;
series, new entries always come with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.760
fair share of interesting details. So it's no&nbsp;
surprise that "No Time to Die" is a layered film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.840
that draws from ideas, imagery, and themes from&nbsp;
the series as a whole. Here's a look at a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.480
small details you may have missed.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.920
1969's "On Her Majesty's Secret Service"&nbsp;
was the last Bond film to genuinely give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.520
Bond hope for true love, in the form of Countess&nbsp;
Tracy di Vicenzo. That film famously ended with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.120
a shocking and heartbreaking death, but Tracy&nbsp;
and Madeleine Swann have quite a bit in common.&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.760
Like Madeleine, Tracy was first introduced to Bond&nbsp;
as the daughter of one of the film's villains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.040
Also as with Madeleine and Bond, Tracy&nbsp;
and Bond's initial intermingling during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.120
a mission transformed into true love. But in "On&nbsp;
Her Majesty's Secret Service," that romance leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.800
to a marriage that sadly ends with Blofeld&nbsp;
having Tracy murdered, right by Bond's side.&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.120
That unforgettable doomed romance has haunted&nbsp;
Bond fans for decades, so going into "No Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.080
to Die" it was natural to worry Madeleine&nbsp;
might face the same fate Tracy once did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.440
The film itself makes countless references to&nbsp;
"On Her Majesty's Secret Service," particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.920
through the use of Louis Armstrong's "We Have&nbsp;
All the Time in the World" theme in the score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.840
as well as having Bond once again say the phrase.
 
"Now we have all the time in the world."
 &nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.200
But this time, rather than Bond saying it&nbsp;
to Tracy right before her vicious murder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.480
it's Bond saying it to Madeleine as he prepares&nbsp;
to sacrifice himself in the film's climax.&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.360
It's a reversal sure to wet every eye in&nbsp;
the house, but in a way, it's a touching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.960
turnabout. Tracy's death put a bit of a pall over&nbsp;
the franchise. But here, with Daniel Craig hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.320
up the tux for good, Bond doesn't have to survive&nbsp;
losing the love of his life. He's able to die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.800
serving something larger than himself.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.480
On a lighter note, "No Time to Die"&nbsp;
reveals something new about one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.200
the franchise's most beloved characters. As&nbsp;
is common with Craig's iteration of Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.040
there is a point in the film's second act when&nbsp;
he must go rogue, disobey his orders from M and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.280
enlist the help of Q in better understanding&nbsp;
the film's MacGuffin, Project Heracles.&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.040
But when Bond and Moneypenny barge&nbsp;
in on Q after hours at his home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.800
he's in the middle of preparing dinner for an&nbsp;
unnamed guest. Given Q's nervousness and general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.760
flustered attitude about finishing this dinner&nbsp;
and not being interrupted by Bond's shenanigans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.200
it stands to reason the guest in question is&nbsp;
a romantic partner of some sort. When Bond and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.800
Moneypenny give him some playful ribbing about who&nbsp;
he's getting so fancy for, he remarks that he will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:45.040
be here any minute, implying Q is not straight.
Where exactly he lies on the sexual orientation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:50.000
spectrum is left unspecified, but alongside&nbsp;
the film's overall better treatment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.840
its female characters, including casting&nbsp;
Lashana Lynch as Bond's 007 replacement Nomi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.640
the casual assertion that Q is queer is a&nbsp;
charming bit of onscreen representation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.280
It bears no heavy impact on the plot, but&nbsp;
it's snuck in admirably matter-of-factly.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.480
While the Daniel Craig iteration of James Bond&nbsp;
is canonically an orphan and an only child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:16.400
"No Time to Die" features him watching two of his&nbsp;
"brothers" die, with very different reactions.&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.040
In "Spectre," we witnessed the controversial&nbsp;
choice to retcon the Christoph Waltz version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.320
of Blofeld to be Bond's adopted brother, the&nbsp;
son of a man who took James in at a young age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.080
It adds an extra twinge of pathos to their feud,&nbsp;
but it goes largely under-explored in that film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.840
and this one, beyond continuing Blofeld's ominous&nbsp;
speeches about his machinations and grudges.&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.840
So it's no surprise that when Bond, while overcome&nbsp;
with rage and choking Blofeld through prison bars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.400
unknowingly exposes his nemesis to the&nbsp;
nanobot bioweapon that will take his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.320
his reaction is mild to say the least.
But he loses another brother earlier in the film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.600
Felix Leiter, the CIA operative who introduced&nbsp;
himself all the way back in "Casino Royale" as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.800
"a brother from Langley."
 
"And what about the winnings?"

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.734
"Does it look like we need the money?"

00:04:06.734 --> 00:04:09.600
Though much of their relationship has taken place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.840
offscreen, with Leiter not appearing in "Skyfall"&nbsp;
or "Spectre," given the amount of time that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.640
passed in the franchise's world, we know Bond and&nbsp;
Leiter were close. They weren't just work friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.920
but rather brothers in arms who had clearly&nbsp;
experienced a lot, on the job and off. Leiter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.000
was one of the only people in these films&nbsp;
who could genuinely call Bond a friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.680
so the brother designation is not far off.
Leiter and Bond wind up on a sinking ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.920
with Leiter suffering from gunshot wounds at&nbsp;
the hands of Logan Ash, a fellow CIA officer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.400
who betrayed them both. The final moments they&nbsp;
share, as they both have to accept Leiter will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.760
never make it off this boat, is a much richer&nbsp;
and more affecting death than Blofeld's.&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.520
But there's a lot of death in "No Time&nbsp;
to Die." None bigger than the last one.

